  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ダイジョ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%80%E3%82%A4%E3%82%B8%E3%83%A7",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106366634,
      "text": "ダイジョ（大薯、ダイショ）は、ヤムイモ（ヤマノイモ目ヤマノイモ科ヤマノイモ属の食用種）の1種の芋類。学名 Dioscorea alata。タメイモ（為芋）、シンショ（参薯）、デンショ（田薯）、コウシャイモ（拳薯）、オキナワヤマイモ（沖縄山芋）、タイワンヤマイモ（台湾山芋）、ウベ (フィリピン語：ube)、パープルヤム 、ウォーターヤム とも。奄美方言ではこうしゃまんという。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Dioscorea alata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioscorea_alata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1377984196,
      "text": "Dioscorea alata – also called ube, ubi, uwhi, purple yam, winged yam, or greater yam, among many other names – is a species of yam. The tubers are usually a vivid violet-purple to bright lavender, but some range from creamy-white to plain white. Although it is sometimes confused with taro and the Okinawa sweet potato beniimo , D. alata is also grown in Okinawa. Its origins are in the Asian and Oceanian tropics. Some varieties attain great size. A mambatap greater yam grown in Maprik, East Sepik Province, Papua New Guinea, around 1939 was 3.5 m (11 ft) in length."
    }
  },
